  <h2>While it's still raining</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Make it safe.</strong> If water is dripping near a light fixture, ceiling fan or outlet, switch off that circuit at the breaker panel, as long as you can reach the panel without standing in water.</li>
    <li><strong>Clear the area.</strong> Move furniture, electronics and rugs out from under the leak, and cover what you can't move with plastic sheeting.</li>
    <li><strong>Catch the water.</strong> Buckets, trash cans and towels. Lay a towel inside the bucket to stop splashing.</li>
    <li><strong>Relieve a bulging ceiling.</strong> If water is pooling above the drywall, puncture the lowest point with a screwdriver over a bucket, standing to the side. If it's sagging or cracking, keep everyone out of the room.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the attic</strong> if you can get there safely. Put a board across the joists and a bucket under the drip, and pull wet insulation away from the ceiling.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>After the storm</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Look from the ground first.</strong> Use binoculars to spot missing shingles, damaged flashing around the chimney and vents, or debris on the roof.</li>
    <li><strong>Get the roof covered.</strong> A tarp secured well past the damaged area keeps the next rain out. Emergency tarping is a reasonable temporary repair your insurer should expect.</li>
    <li><strong>Dry the inside.</strong> Wet attic insulation usually has to be removed. Ceiling drywall that sagged or stained deeply usually comes out too. Attic framing and roof sheathing need to dry quickly to prevent mold and rot.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Document for insurance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Video and photos of the ceiling, walls, floors and damaged belongings, and of the roof from the ground.</li>
    <li>The date and time the leak started and the weather that day. Local storm reports can support a wind or hail claim.</li>
    <li>Receipts for tarps, buckets, fans, emergency services and any hotel stay.</li>
    <li>Don't throw away damaged shingles or belongings until the adjuster has seen them or approved disposal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common roof leak sources</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Clue</th></tr></thead>
